cnet.device 1.9 fixed - NE2000 PCMCIA cards that carry DECnet
==============================================================

This is cnet.device 1.9 by Bruce Abbott, Harry "Piru" Sintonen and
Rolf Anders (Aminet driver/net/cnetdevice.lha, public domain) with two
bugs fixed, one byte each. Without the fixes DECnet does not work on
these cards; TCP/IP is not affected, because it keeps the card's own
Ethernet address.

1. S2_CONFIGINTERFACE checks the new Ethernet address with a signed
   test ("ble"). Every address that starts with a byte of 80 (hex) or
   more counts as invalid, DECnet's AA-00-04-00-xx-xx among them. The
   driver then refuses the address and, after that, every read and
   write. Fixed: only an all-zero address is refused ("beq").

2. When the last program closes the driver, it stops and resets the
   card but still remembers the address as set. The next program that
   opens it gets the card started with the card's own address, and its
   S2_CONFIGINTERFACE is refused as "already configured". The card then
   sends with the new address but receives nothing sent to it. With
   DECnet this happens after dncheck (the installer runs it before
   DECnet-Start) and after DECnet-Stop and DECnet-Start: DN: stays
   empty after a long wait. Fixed: closing also forgets the address,
   so the next program can set it again.

Tested on a real A1200 (Blizzard 1230-IV, OS 3.2.3) with a D-Link
DE-660+, cnet.device (68020+ version): dncheck, DECnet-Start, DN: read
and write, DECnet-Stop and Start, 7 MB read (about 156 KB/s) and
written back unchanged on 10BASE-T; dncopy on BNC as well. The card
picks the port by itself, even while DECnet runs. cnet.device.000
(68000 version) passed dncheck on the same machine. cnet16.device froze
the A1200 with this card as soon as it was opened, before any output;
it was not tried further. The other versions carry the same two bytes
but have not been tried on real hardware yet.


Installing
----------
Pick the same version you would take from the Aminet archive and copy
it over DEVS:Networks/cnet.device or DEVS:Networks/cnet16.device; keep
the old file. Mind the name: each file opens only under its own device
name, cnet.device for the left column, cnet16.device for the right one
(cnet16.device copied to cnet.device gives error -1).

  cnet.device            68020 and up       cnet16.device
  cnet.device.000        68000/68010        cnet16.device.000
  cnet.device.turboio    68020 and up,      cnet16.device.turboio
                         faster I/O
  cnet.device.debug      debug output       cnet16.device.debug
                         (Sashimi)

cnet16.device is for cards that need 16-bit register reads (mostly
Fast Ethernet cards). For the D-Link DE-660+ take cnet.device:
cnet16.device froze the machine with it. The fixed files say
"1.9 (15.11.26)" in Version.

The driver takes the PCMCIA slot when it is first opened and gives it
back only when it leaves memory. To try another version without a
reboot, stop all network software and run "Avail FLUSH" first, or the
new one gets error -1.

On an A1200 without the PCMCIA reset modification, CardReset (Aminet
util/boot/CardReset.lha) may be needed before the card opens; see
cnet.guide in the Aminet archive.


Contents
--------
  Devs/Networks/    all eight versions, fixed
  cnetpatch.py      makes them from the Aminet files (Python 3):
                      cnetpatch.py IN OUT
                    it finds both places by their code, so it fits
                    every version, and refuses a file where it does not
  cnetfix.patch     the same two changes in the source (src/cnetdevice.asm
                    of the Aminet archive)

The fixed files were made with cnetpatch.py from the Aminet binaries,
not assembled again. The source and the rest of the original archive
(guide, compatibility list, examples) are in driver/net/cnetdevice.lha
on Aminet.

cnet.device is public domain. This fixed version is not an official
release by its authors.
